
  [1mB. Domain event log + fan-out worker.[0m Mutations append one row to an [38;5;153mevents[39m table. A worker reads events, resolves
  subscribers, and writes notifications and emails.
  [3mWins:[0m subscriber logic lives in exactly one place; you get an audit trail and can replay events after a bug or a
  subscription-rule change. [3mCosts:[0m eventual consistency — the badge lags the change by however often the worker runs,
  which people notice and report as a bug; a worker that must always be running or notifications stop with no visible
  error.

  [1mC. No notification rows — derive the feed at read time.[0m Store a [38;5;153mtask_changes[39m history only. The feed is a join: changes
  to tasks I'm subscribed to, newer than my [38;5;153mlast_read_at[39m. Unread count is a [38;5;153mCOUNT(*)[39m.
  [3mWins:[0m fewest tables, no fan-out, no worker for the in-app side, impossible to get out of sync because nothing is
  duplicated. [3mCosts:[0m read state is one watermark per user, so no dismissing an individual item and no per-notification
  state ever; email still needs its own outbox, so you end up with two mechanisms anyway.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m B's audit trail and replay are real benefits, but they're solving for a scale and a rate of
  rule-churn you don't have, and it buys them with a lagging badge — the single most visible quality defect in a
  notification feature. C is genuinely tempting on YAGNI grounds, and I'd pick it if this were in-app only; but you've
  chosen email, which forces an outbox and a worker regardless, so C's main saving evaporates while you still lose
  per-item read state permanently. A gives the correct, instant, dismissible feed for one extra table, and its real risk
  — a mutation path that forgets to emit — is fixable by construction: route all writes through a single [38;5;153mtask_service[39m
  module and it can't be bypassed by accident.
